The best approach is to adopt the **`System.IO.Abstractions`** NuGet package — it's battle-tested and already provides `IFileSystem` with mocking support.

## Setup

```bash
dotnet add package System.IO.Abstractions
dotnet add <YourTestProject> package System.IO.Abstractions.TestingHelpers
```

## Register in DI

```csharp
builder.Services.AddSingleton<IFileSystem, FileSystem>();
```

## Inject into your classes

```csharp
public class ConfigService(IFileSystem fileSystem)
{
    public string Load(string path)
        => fileSystem.File.ReadAllText(path);

    public void Save(string path, string content)
        => fileSystem.File.WriteAllText(path, content);

    public bool PathExists(string dir)
        => fileSystem.Directory.Exists(dir);
}
```

## Test with MockFileSystem

```csharp
var mockFs = new MockFileSystem(new Dictionary<string, MockFileData>
{
    { "/data/config.json", new MockFileData("{\"key\": \"value\"}") }
});

var service = new ConfigService(mockFs);

Assert.Equal("{\"key\": \"value\"}", service.Load("/data/config.json"));

service.Save("/data/output.txt", "hello");
Assert.Equal("hello", mockFs.File.ReadAllText("/data/output.txt"));

Assert.True(service.PathExists("/data"));
Assert.False(service.PathExists("/nope"));
```

## Why this over a custom wrapper?

| | Custom `IFileService` | `System.IO.Abstractions` |
|---|---|---|
| Coverage | Only methods you wrap | Full `System.IO` surface |
| Maintenance | You own it | Community-maintained |
| Test helpers | You build them | `MockFileSystem` included |

**TL;DR:** Install the package, register `IFileSystem` as a singleton, inject it where you currently call `File.*`/`Directory.*`, and use `MockFileSystem` in tests. Once registered, you can use the `migrate-static-to-wrapper` skill to bulk-replace all existing call sites.
